class Node {
    int data;
    Node left, right;

    public Node(int d) {
        data = d;
        left = right = null;
    }
}

class Solution {
    HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();                                     // stores visited node values

    boolean findTarget(Node root, int target) {                                 // main recursive check function
        if(root == null){ return false; }                                      // empty node, no pair possible

        if(set.contains(target - root.data)){                                  // complement already seen
            return true;
        }

        set.add(root.data);                                                    // mark current value as visited

        return findTarget(root.left,target) || findTarget(root.right, target); // recurse into both subtrees
    }
}

//////////////////////// DRY RUN /////////////////////////////

root = [7, 3, 8, 2, 4, N, 9], target = 12

            7
           / \
          3   8
         / \   \
        2   4   9

Visit 7  →  set = {7}
Visit 3  →  set = {7,3}
Visit 2  →  set = {7,3,2}
Visit 4  →  need 8  →  not in set  →  set = {7,3,2,4}
Visit 8  →  need 4  →  found in set  →  return True

//////////////////////// QUESTION ////////////////////////////

Pair Sum in BST

Given a Binary Search Tree(BST) and a target. Check whether there's a pair of Nodes in the BST with value summing up to the target.

Example 1:

            7
           / \
          3   8
         / \   \
        2   4   9

Input: root[] = [7, 3, 8, 2, 4, N, 9], target = 12
Output: True
Explanation: In the binary tree above, there are two nodes (8 and 4) that add up to 12.

Example 2:

             9
            / \
           5   10
          / \    \
         2   6    12

Input: root[] = [9, 5, 10, 2, 6, N, 12], target = 23
Output: False
Explanation: In the binary tree above, there are no such two nodes exists that add up to 23.

Constraints:
1 ≤ |root[i]|, target ≤ 10^6
